telling him that poland is  sleep walking into exit from the eu  would you agree with that  characterization  well um i don't think that it's really a  realistic option  because as we just heard  eu is  the us as a whole is still very popular  in poland and that is something that the  polish government knows very well  but  the problem is that it just cannot  continue um this dispute  is dividing the european union and there  are so many important challenges ahead  just like think about the climate change  or the fallout of the coronavirus  pandemic so  member states they need each other and  after this recent decision by the  european court of justice that ordered  poland to pay one million euros per day  for um  challenging the rule of law  when we talk in general terms  that is why i think that  now there is a chance that the polish  government could come up with a small  solution  face saving compromise to avoid those  fines and  to  try to  you know to have a solution to this  problem at least for now  and andreas where do you think things go  from here as as you said in your opening  statement the eu actually has no formal  mechanism for throwing out a member  state do you think that this hefty fine  that has been imposed and it's not the  only fine by the way that is confronting  poland do you think that that could  concentrate some mines in warsaw and  lead leaders there to take a kind of a  an exit ramp from the conflict rather  than from the eu itself well first of  all unfortunately the default  approach in the by the european union to  any problem is a sort of muddling  through  uh rather than a decisive uh dealing  with any any issue so i think we're  probably in for some muddling through on  the brussels side  on the warsaw side um  i'm less sure but the the positive  scenario to to solving the the whole  tangled hairball of the problem is that  in hungary next year at an election next  year and in poland at an election in two  years  that the voters there they still they  are compromised democracies but they're  still democracies and if polls and  hungarians decide look  we're tired of this we want to stay that  they vote these governments out then  then we will have remove the blockage  and then reform the club so that it can  expel members in future  if it becomes necessary again and let me  ask you about that uh prospect of  poland of course still does have  elections and um  the fact is it is the biggest net  recipient of eu largesse and it also  stands to benefit  quite massively from the eu pandemic  recovery fund next generation eu  so  what would polish citizens do if in fact  that financial tap  is closed off do you think there would  be uh the peace party would be kicked  out of office  um  i think that the peace party would try  to blame brussels and blame germany and  blame everybody in the world or in the  european union for  uh not giving poland money and for  keeping money which should be paid to to  poland on the other hand  there would be a  big  there will be protests in poland because  we know there are a lot of people who  want to stay in the european union  people in poland would not let this  really happen that the government would  somehow uh pull poland out of the eu but  of course the government says they don't  want to do it because that's that's not  what they are aiming at they want to  stay in the european union but what they  are indirectly doing by uh by  challenging the the most fundamental  laws this is somehow exiting the  european uh common uh common law uh uh  uh environment uh so this is what what  the government this is what the  government is is doing but uh it's hard  to say what will really happen if there  is no more money will the government be  able to convince people it's the fault  of the eu or will the people in poland  see no it is the government which did  the bad job that's why we're not getting  this money which poland really needs and  even the government needs this money  very very much  meanwhile on poland's eastern border  europe's uh current uh currently worst  migration crisis is playing out poland  has been pushing back sometimes  violently against a mass influx of  middle eastern refugees orchestrated by  neighboring belarus  this syrian family has been stranded in  a forest in the border area between  belarus and poland aid workers provided  them with makeshift supplies but that  kind of help is illegal poland has  closed the border area to aid workers  and journalists so the eight workers  don't want to be identified  i've seen a lot of bad things during my  career but not this kind of human  tragedy these people are sitting in the  forest are hungry and half frozen to  death and are pleading for help  organizations say polish soldiers are  forcing refugees back to belarus such  so-called pushbacks are illegal under eu  law but according to polish law they're  now allowed  it's something that  you don't wish for your worst enemy  we've been sleeping in the woods for the  past four days  it's it's  it's like a nightmare  poland is securing the 400 kilometer  border with barbed wire and will later  replace it with a wall  still many refugees have been reaching  germany  interior minister horst zeihofer has  tightened border controls and does not  disagree with poland's planned wall  construction  do the eu partners approve of poland's  actions on its eastern border  and let me take that question straight  to alexandra the eu is happy to sanction  poland when it comes to interfering with  the judiciary but remarkably silent  about the very questionable actions  being taken on that border to belarus  well that is true and the criticism that  has been expressed  doesn't bring any results  commissioner johansen traveled to poland  she offered poland support front texts  uh that frontex should be  participating in what is going on at the  border to belarus security guard yeah  yeah thank you for explaining that uh  sorry uh yes and and uh poland didn't  want them uh to come and so there is  some criticism uh  poland is facing for what it is doing at  the border with belarus for pushing back  migrants uh back to belarus for not  letting journalists and ngos enter the  zone that is covered by the emergency  state however when we talk  about the support among member states  this is pretty much there many member  states say  it is important to protect  our external borders we need to support  poland we cannot let lukashenko uh the  belarusian  belarusian dictator  we cannot let him send migrants into the  european union that needs to stop and  that is why we need to support poland  mr lukashenko is doing this essentially  to take revenge against europe for the  sanctions it has imposed on its regime  is the eu leaving poland in the lurch by  not standing up to likoshenko more  forcefully  well if polish diplomacy was  maybe able to act more actively in the  european union and in the world maybe it  would be able to create some more  pressure on uh kashenko and also  probably on russia because  with i think it's not only belarus maybe  russia also involved in this in this  operation but as we've we've heard  poland  refuse the help from this european  security agency poland wants to try to  secure the border uh on its own so i  don't hear voices in poland saying now  oh european union help us  that's not the narrative now  let me step back uh and uh ask you  andreas for a wider perspective on this  angela merkel will soon be stepping down  and there are voices saying that just at  this moment when europe  faces multiple crises that it is  leaderless do you think that's right  that europe is leaderless yes i think  that's um i think it was always  exaggerated how much merkel was the  leader of europe um but  some people i mean emmanuel macron  president of france would like to  present himself as the leader but he's  not being accepted as such by the other  nations mario draghi prime minister of  italy has enormous gravitas from his  time as ecb a governor is very  pro-american very good in english but  could play that role but in general i  think you're right it probably looks  like europe will remain leaderless for  some time  let me come back to our title we have  just uh about one minute left on the  clock but i want to just put that  question out there again uh will poll  exit follow brexit what's your guess  alexandra  i don't think so  i know that we will be dealing with this  problem for a longer time but i don't  think that uh poland will decide to  leave the european union i don't think  so but let's talk about it maybe in one  and a half or two years after elections  in hungary in poland  okay i agree that poll exit is not the  danger the danger is a muddling through  and a const continual hollowing out of  the european union and its values and  its cons and constitutional setup  thank you very much to all of you for  this very uh interesting exchange and  thanks to all of you out there for  tuning in see you soon  [Music]  you
 

